  "hints": [
    "Re-sorting every score after each add is wasteful. Which scores can never be the k-th largest again?",
    "Only the k largest scores matter. Keep them in a min-heap of size k: its smallest element is the k-th largest overall.",
    "On add, push the score, and if the heap now holds more than k items pop the minimum. Return the heap's top."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "class Leaderboard {\n  constructor(k, nums) {\n    this.k = k\n    this.heap = []\n    for (const num of nums) this.add(num)\n  }\n\n  add(score) {\n    const heap = this.heap\n    if (heap.length < this.k) {\n      heap.push(score)\n      let i = heap.length - 1\n      while (i > 0) {\n        const parent = (i - 1) >> 1\n        if (heap[parent] <= heap[i]) break\n        ;[heap[parent], heap[i]] = [heap[i], heap[parent]]\n        i = parent\n      }\n    } else if (score > heap[0]) {\n      heap[0] = score\n      let i = 0\n      for (;;) {\n        const l = 2 * i + 1\n        const r = l + 1\n        let m = i\n        if (l < heap.length && heap[l] < heap[m]) m = l\n        if (r < heap.length && heap[r] < heap[m]) m = r\n        if (m === i) break\n        ;[heap[m], heap[i]] = [heap[i], heap[m]]\n        i = m\n      }\n    }\n    return heap[0]\n  }\n}\n\nfunction runLeaderboard(k, nums, adds) {\n  const board = new Leaderboard(k, nums)\n  return adds.map(score => board.add(score))\n}\n",
    "py": "import heapq\n\n\nclass Leaderboard:\n    def __init__(self, k, nums):\n        self.k = k\n        self.heap = []\n        for num in nums:\n            self.add(num)\n\n    def add(self, score):\n        if len(self.heap) < self.k:\n            heapq.heappush(self.heap, score)\n        elif score > self.heap[0]:\n            heapq.heapreplace(self.heap, score)\n        return self.heap[0]\n\n\ndef run_leaderboard(k, nums, adds):\n    board = Leaderboard(k, nums)\n    return [board.add(score) for score in adds]\n",
    "explanation": "A min-heap holding the k largest scores keeps the k-th largest at its root. A new score smaller than or equal to the root cannot enter the top k, so it is ignored; otherwise it replaces the root and sifts down. Each add costs O(log k)."
  },
